<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liu Jin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1451–<a href="/years/1510">1510</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liu Jin was a Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> who dominated the government of the Zhengde Emperor from 1506 to 1510. Castrated as a boy and adopted by a palace eunuch, he served the future emperor from 1492 as one of the eunuchs later called the Eight Tigers. As head of the Directorate of Ceremonial, the most influential eunuch office, he pressed officials with fear and fines to raise state revenue, raised eunuch intendants to equal footing with provincial officials, and changed the examination quotas in favour of the north. His reforms provoked unrest and mutinies; in 1510 the other Tigers accused him of treason, and he was executed by slow slicing.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A few rose to rule the court, as Wang Zhen, <b>Liu Jin</b> and Wei Zhongxian did.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
